 and particularly by the work of Guillaume Farel and later John Calvin, the city became a leading center of Calvinism and a refuge for Protestants from across Europe. Politically, Geneva developed republican institutions modeled on some of the Swiss cities, asserting its sovereignty against the Duchy of Savoy and navigating a precarious position between powerful neighbors such as France and the Swiss cantons. \nFrom its declaration of independence in 1534 until its annexation by revolutionary France in 1798, Geneva evolved into a prosperous hub of trade, finance, watchmaking, and publishing, while maintaining a distinctive identity as both a fortified “Protestant Rome” and a cosmopolitan republic."}],"facts":[{"qid":"Q23366230","kind":"capital","value_qid":"Q71","value_label":"Geneva","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P36","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Geneva"},{"qid":"Q23366230","kind":"official_religion","value_qid":"Q101849","value_label":"Reformed Christianity","from_t":1555,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P3075","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Reformed Christianity"},{"qid":"Q23366230","kind":"predecessor","value_qid":"Q2159313","value_label":"Prince-Bishopric of Geneva","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P155","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Prince-Bishopric of Geneva"},{"qid":"Q23366230","kind":"successor","value_qid":"Q11917","value_label":"Canton of Geneva","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P1366","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Canton of Geneva"},{"qid":"Q23366230","kind":"successor","value_qid":"Q691368","value_label":"Léman (department)","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P1366","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Léman (department)"}],"people":{},"media":{},"names":["Cenevre Cumhuriyeti","Geneva","Genève","Repubblica di Ginevra","Republic of Geneva","Republiek van Genève","Republik Genf","República de Ginebra","République de Genève","res publica Genavensis","Δημοκρατία της Γενεύης","Женевск